					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/rockteethmotheyes" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(4022320); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=4022320&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1607200748" alt="RockTeethMothEyes" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=4022320)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/rockteethmotheyes" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(4022320); return false;" >RockTeethMothEyes</a></span> <span class="odate time_1557487119 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">10 May 2019 11:18</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4244340">
									

<p>This was a tough vote for me for a few reasons.</p>
<p>I enjoy the writing here. It's very expressive, the way you detail everyone's manner of speech, their composure, and how driven by the circumstances of their unique loss. It's very touching at times. Knowing what its like to lose someone that has always been in your life, I can resonate with some of these people. It's good.</p>
<p>However, it's mostly fluff. I only feel this connection because I understand the pain in the way they write it out, but it's <em>because</em> of the way they write it out that I can't truly empathize with them. They aren't real. No one talks like this, not even in severe mental degradation. No one just spouts about their tragic loss when locked in a room for 15 minutes. I think this could have been improved upon if there wasn't so much being written or said by the characters. Less is more.</p>
<p>In short, I guess what I'm saying is that I downvoted because the compulsion didn't make these people real.</p>

							</div>

								
